</section>

<section style="padding:4rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="max-width:800px;">
      <p style="font-size:0.6rem;letter-spacing:0.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e00;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Styles &amp; Fits</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">T-Shirt Styles We Produce</h2>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:1rem;">Crew neck is the default and the pattern we have most refined — ribbed 1x1 collar, set-in sleeve, standard drop-shoulder or fitted grading depending on your size spec. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">V-neck</strong> needs accurate rib-tape mitering at the point, which we flag at the tech pack stage since it's a common failure point on lower-tier factories. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">Raglan</strong> and <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">dolman</strong> sleeves change the shoulder seam construction entirely and usually take an extra sampling round to get the armhole right the first time you run them with us.</p>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;">On fit, we run <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">boxy and oversized</strong> streetwear blocks with dropped shoulders and a squared hem, standard retail fit for general apparel brands, and <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">fitted or muscle-fit</strong> grading with side seams for activewear-adjacent labels. Long sleeve and tank variations use the same base block with sleeve or neckline swapped, so if you already have an approved crew neck pattern with us, adding a long sleeve version to the line doesn't require a full re-grade.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding:4rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="max-width:800px;">
      <p style="font-size:0.6rem;letter-spacing:0.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e00;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Construction Guide</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">T-Shirt Fabric &amp; Weight Guide</h2>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:1rem;"><strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">Single jersey</strong> is the standard t-shirt knit — smooth face, light stretch, the base for most basic tees. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">Combed ringspun</strong> cotton uses a finer, more uniform yarn than carded cotton, giving a softer hand-feel and cleaner print surface — the usual choice for premium fashion tees. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">Piqué</strong> knit has a slightly textured, waffle-like surface and is more common on polo-style shirts, while <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">slub</strong> jersey has an intentionally irregular yarn thickness for a worn-in, vintage look.</p>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:1rem;">GSM (grams per square meter) is the main lever for how a t-shirt feels and drapes. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">140–160gsm</strong> is lightweight — drapey, good for hot climates and fashion-forward fits. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">180–200gsm</strong> is the most common mid-weight range for everyday retail tees, balancing structure and comfort. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">200–220gsm</strong> is heavyweight — the streetwear-standard boxy, structured feel that holds screen prints and embroidery especially well.</p>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;">Neckline finishing affects both look and durability. A ribbed crew neck (1x1 or 2x1 rib) is the standard, cost-effective choice. Banded necklines (self-fabric folded rather than separate ribbing) give a cleaner, more premium look but add a construction step.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding:4rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="max-width:800px;">
      <p style="font-size:0.6rem;letter-spacing:0.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e00;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Decoration</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Printing &amp; Embellishment Options</h2>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:1rem;"><strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">Screen printing</strong> is done in-house and is the most cost-effective option once you're above roughly 100 pieces per design — each additional color adds a screen and a setup step, so 1-2 color designs price noticeably better than full-color art. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">Embroidery</strong> is also in-house, standard for logo placements on chest, sleeve, or back yoke. See our <a href="/printing-service/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">printing service</a> page for setup costs and turnaround by method.</p>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;">For <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">DTF</strong> and <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">sublimation</strong> all-over prints, we route through partner facilities we manage directly — you still deal with one point of contact and we sign off on quality before anything ships, but lead time runs a few days longer than in-house work. DTF tends to suit smaller runs and gradient-heavy or photographic designs better than screen print does. If you're unsure which method fits your design, our <a href="/insights/printing-methods-compared/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">printing methods comparison</a> breaks down cost and durability by technique.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding:4rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="max-width:800px;">
      <p style="font-size:0.6rem;letter-spacing:0.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e00;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Order Types</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Private Label, OEM, and Blank Wholesale</h2>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:1rem;">Most brands ordering t-shirts from us go the <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">private label</strong> route: your neck labels, hang tags, and packaging on our production, sourced through our label suppliers or yours if you already have one set up. <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">OEM</strong> production follows your tech pack and spec sheet exactly, no design input needed on our end. If you're supplying your own fabric and only need cut-make-trim, that's a separate <strong style="color:#fff;font-weight:500;">CMT</strong> arrangement with a 5,000-piece minimum rather than the 300-piece OEM/ODM MOQ, since sourcing and dyeing a fabric lot for a single client doesn't scale below that volume.</p>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;">Not sure which model fits your brand yet? Our <a href="/insights/oem-vs-odm-vs-cmt/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">OEM vs ODM vs CMT comparison</a> and <a href="/white-label-manufacturer/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">private label vs white label guide</a> walk through the practical differences before you commit to a tech pack.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding:4rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="max-width:800px;">
      <p style="font-size:0.6rem;letter-spacing:0.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e00;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Getting Started</p>
      <h2 style="font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Sampling, Sizing &amp; Lead Time</h2>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:1rem;">A proto sample typically takes 10-14 days once we have your tech pack, and up to 10 free revision rounds are included before bulk cutting starts — most clients need 2-3 to lock in fit and fabric. Full size runs are graded against your spec sheet or, if you don't have one yet, our <a href="/insights/international-size-chart-guide/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">size chart guide</a> covering US, UK, and EU conversions. Bulk production runs 30-45 days after you sign off on the pre-production sample, and every order goes through AQL 2.5 inspection before shipment.</p>
      <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.9;">Labeling and packaging — woven neck labels, printed care labels, hang tags, poly bags — get sourced and set up during the sampling phase. Our <a href="/garment-labels/types-of-garment-labels/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">garment labels guide</a> covers the label types worth knowing before you brief your first order.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding:4rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;">
  <div class="container">
    <p style="font-size:0.6rem;letter-spacing:0.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#e00;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Common Questions</p>
    <h2 style="font-size:1.4rem;font-weight:300;color:#fff;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">T-Shirt Manufacturer FAQ</h2>
    <div style="max-width:800px;">
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">What's the minimum order for a t-shirt style?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">300 pieces per style for OEM and ODM orders, split across sizes and up to 3 colors within that style. Blank CMT orders where you supply the fabric run at a 5,000-piece minimum — sourcing and dyeing a fabric lot for one client doesn't scale efficiently below that. If 300 is still more than you need to start, see our <a href="/small-batch-t-shirt-manufacturer/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">small batch t-shirt manufacturer</a> page.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">What GSM should I choose for my t-shirt line?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">It depends on positioning and climate. 140-160gsm suits hot-climate retail and fashion-forward drapey fits; 180-200gsm is the safest default for general retail; 200-220gsm suits streetwear brands wanting a boxier, more structured feel. We can send physical swatches at multiple weights before you commit.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Can you produce private label t-shirts with my own brand tags?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Yes. Private label is the default setup for most t-shirt clients — your neck labels, hang tags, and packaging, our production. We handle woven label sourcing and can work from your existing label supplier if you already have one.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">What printing and decoration methods do you offer?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Screen printing and embroidery in-house. DTF and sublimation for all-over prints through partner facilities we manage directly, so you still deal with one point of contact and one quality sign-off. Screen print is the more cost-effective choice above roughly 100 pieces per design; DTF suits smaller runs or gradient-heavy artwork.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">What's the difference between carded and combed ringspun cotton?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Combed ringspun cotton goes through an extra combing process that removes shorter fibers, producing a finer, more even yarn — softer hand-feel and a cleaner surface for printing, at a moderate cost premium over standard carded cotton.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-bottom:1px solid #e00;padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">How long does an order take from sample approval to shipment?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">30-45 days for bulk production after you approve the pre-production sample, depending on order size, print complexity, and current factory load. Proto and fit samples before that typically take 10-14 days to develop, with up to 10 free revision rounds included.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="padding:1.5rem 0;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;color:#fff;margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Do heavier GSM tees cost significantly more?</h3>
        <p style="color:#FFFFFF;font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.8;">Heavier fabric uses more raw material per garment, so cost rises with GSM, but the increase from 180gsm to 220gsm is usually a modest per-piece difference rather than a dramatic one — fiber content (standard cotton vs. GOTS organic vs. blends) typically has a bigger cost impact than the weight jump itself. Our <a href="/clothing-manufacturing-cost-guide/" style="color:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid #e00;text-decoration:none;">manufacturing cost guide</a> breaks down what drives price per unit in more depth.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

